Chromosomal deletion syndromes result from deletion of parts of chromosomes. Depending on the location, size, and whom the deletion is inhered from, there are a few known different variations of chromosome deletions. Chromosomal deletion syndromes typically involve larger deletions that are visible using karyotyping techniques. Smaller deletions result in Microdeletion syndrome, which are detected using fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH)

Some examples of chromosomal deletion syndromes include 5p-Deletion (cri du chat syndrome), 4p-Deletion (Wolf–Hirschhorn syndrome), Prader–Willi syndrome, and Angelman syndrome.

The chromosomal basis of Cri du chat syndrome consists of a deletion of the most terminal portion of the short arm of chromosome 5. 5p deletions, whether terminal or interstitial, occur at different breakpoints; the chromosomal basis generally consists of a deletion on the short arm of chromosome 5. The variability seen among individuals may be attributed to the differences in their genotypes. With an uniparental genome analysis, it was shown that 75% of children with Cri du chat have complete segregation of the mutation responsible for the disorder. Deletion of any chromosome is associated with increased risk of various neurodevelopmental disorders, including Down's syndrome and autism spectrum disorders.

Some examples of the possible dysmorphic features include:
• Down's syndrome - narrow foreheads, small noses, broad faces;
• Spina bifida - joint bedsores;
• Giraffe  “deformed” ears;
• Cystic fibrosis - hoarseness, chronic fatigue, recurrent vaginal infections, and skin conditions such as Stevens–Johnson syndrome and dry eyes;
• Fragile X - recurrent sudden loss of toes and fingers;
• Congenital myelomeningocele - muscle weakness and poor posture;
• Huntington's disease - loss of social communication and coordination;
• Schizophrenia - psychotic symptoms, altered emotions, visual disturbances, delusions; language impairment; and autism - difficulty focusing, absentmindedness, irritability, speech impairments; attention deficit hyperactivity.

